meta {
  name: Upload Profile Image to S3
  type: http
  seq: 10
}

post {
  url: {{s3UploadUrl}}
  body: multipartForm
  auth: none
}

body:multipart-form {
  Content-Type: {{s3ContentType}}
  key: {{s3Key}}
  X-Amz-Credential: {{s3Credential}}
  X-Amz-Algorithm: {{s3Algorithm}}
  X-Amz-Date: {{s3Date}}
  X-Amz-Security-Token: {{s3SecurityToken}}
  Policy: {{s3Policy}}
  X-Amz-Signature: {{s3Signature}}
  file: @file(/path/to/your/image.jpg)
}

docs {
  ## How to use
  
  1. Run "Get Profile Image Upload URL" first — its post-response script saves all the
     presigned fields into environment variables automatically.
  
  2. Replace `/path/to/your/image.jpg` with the actual path to the image on your machine.
  
  3. Run this request. A 204 (No Content) response from S3 means the upload succeeded.
  
  ## Why so many fields?
  
  S3 presigned POST uses a signed policy embedded in the form fields to verify:
  - who is uploading (Credential + Signature)
  - what they are allowed to upload (Policy: content type, max size, key prefix)
  - when the upload was signed (Date) and expires
  
  All fields must be present and unmodified — changing any of them invalidates the
  signature and S3 will return a 403.
  
  ## Field order matters
  
  AWS S3 requires the `file` field to be the last field in the multipart form.
  Bruno sends fields in the order they are listed here, so do not move `file` up.
}
